1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,063
Section two taught you the failure mode: a chunk boundary cutting through a list or

2
00:00:05,063 --> 00:00:07,732
a table, producing a fragment nobody could use.

3
00:00:07,732 --> 00:00:12,501
This lecture is about the decision underneath that one, the one that gets made once,

4
00:00:12,501 --> 00:00:16,930
early, and quietly shapes every retrieval from then on: how big should a chunk

5
00:00:16,930 --> 00:00:17,611
actually be.

6
00:00:17,611 --> 00:00:19,542
There's no universal right answer.

7
00:00:19,542 --> 00:00:24,255
There's a trade-off, and knowing which side of it your content needs is a genuinely

8
00:00:24,255 --> 00:00:28,740
product decision, not something to leave entirely to whoever's implementing it.

9
00:00:28,740 --> 00:00:31,409
Most teams never have this conversation at all.

10
00:00:31,409 --> 00:00:36,008
Someone picks a chunk size from a tutorial or a default library setting, it works

11
00:00:36,008 --> 00:00:39,188
well enough on the first few test queries, and it ships.

12
00:00:39,188 --> 00:00:43,560
That's not a decision, it's an accident that happened to look fine in a demo.

13
00:00:43,560 --> 00:00:47,762
The gap between accident and decision is exactly what this lecture closes.

14
00:00:48,312 --> 00:00:52,132
Read this as a genuine trade-off, not a right answer and a wrong one.

15
00:00:52,132 --> 00:00:54,014
Neither column is the safe choice.

16
00:00:54,014 --> 00:00:58,332
Small chunks retrieve precisely, and precisely is exactly the problem when the

17
00:00:58,332 --> 00:01:01,543
precise sentence needs a sentence before it to make sense.

18
00:01:01,543 --> 00:01:05,917
Large chunks solve that by keeping more together, and pay for it with retrieval

19
00:01:05,917 --> 00:01:10,069
that's less targeted and a prompt that costs more tokens every single call.

20
00:01:10,069 --> 00:01:12,837
There's no setting that avoids both costs at once.

21
00:01:12,837 --> 00:01:17,210
Choosing where to sit on that line is choosing which failure you'd rather have.

22
00:01:17,210 --> 00:01:18,816
Which side should you favour?

23
00:01:18,816 --> 00:01:22,248
It depends on what your feature actually does with the answer.

24
00:01:22,248 --> 00:01:26,788
A quick factual lookup, a price, a date, a policy number, wants small and precise,

25
00:01:26,788 --> 00:01:31,438
because the whole answer is one fact and padding it with surrounding paragraphs only

26
00:01:31,438 --> 00:01:32,047
adds noise.

27
00:01:32,047 --> 00:01:36,642
A feature explaining a multi-step process wants larger chunks, because cutting that

28
00:01:36,642 --> 00:01:41,182
process into precise but disconnected fragments defeats the point of retrieving it

29
00:01:41,182 --> 00:01:41,569
at all.

30
00:01:42,119 --> 00:01:46,895
Here's the decision most teams skip entirely: your corpus probably isn't one kind of

31
00:01:46,895 --> 00:01:47,406
document.

32
00:01:47,406 --> 00:01:51,670
It's several, and each one has a natural unit that isn't a character count.

33
00:01:51,670 --> 00:01:55,593
An FAQ's natural unit is a question and its answer, together, always.

34
00:01:55,593 --> 00:01:58,151
A policy document's natural unit is a clause.

35
00:01:58,151 --> 00:02:02,472
A table's natural unit is a row, and a row without its header means nothing.

36
00:02:02,472 --> 00:02:07,133
Apply one fixed chunk size to all three and you'll get an FAQ answer with half the

37
00:02:07,133 --> 00:02:11,909
question missing, a policy clause severed from the sentence that qualifies it, and a

38
00:02:11,909 --> 00:02:13,671
table row nobody can interpret.

39
00:02:13,671 --> 00:02:17,878
Three different content types, three different failures, one shared cause.

40
00:02:17,878 --> 00:02:22,539
This is worth raising specifically because it's an easy thing for a PM to actually

41
00:02:22,539 --> 00:02:26,689
catch that an engineer implementing quickly might not think to ask about.

42
00:02:26,689 --> 00:02:28,907
You know what's in your knowledge base.

43
00:02:28,907 --> 00:02:31,920
You know an FAQ looks nothing like a policy document.

44
00:02:31,920 --> 00:02:36,240
That knowledge is the entire input this decision needs, and it's exactly the

45
00:02:36,240 --> 00:02:40,731
knowledge sitting with the product side of the table, not the engineering side.

46
00:02:41,281 --> 00:02:45,736
Say the honest caveat, because the instinct when chunking feels risky is to make

47
00:02:45,736 --> 00:02:49,744
chunks bigger, and that instinct backfires in a specific, technical way.

48
00:02:49,744 --> 00:02:52,306
An embedding represents what a chunk is about.

49
00:02:52,306 --> 00:02:56,816
Cram three unrelated ideas into one chunk and the embedding becomes a blur of all

50
00:02:56,816 --> 00:03:00,713
three, matching none of them as sharply as a focused chunk would have.

51
00:03:00,713 --> 00:03:02,383
Bigger doesn't buy you safety.

52
00:03:02,383 --> 00:03:06,838
It buys you a chunk that's vaguely about everything and precisely about nothing,

53
00:03:06,838 --> 00:03:11,292
which is a worse retrieval result than a well-scoped small chunk that happens to

54
00:03:11,292 --> 00:03:13,185
lack a little surrounding context.

55
00:03:13,185 --> 00:03:17,751
The instinct to go bigger usually comes from a real fear, that a narrow chunk will

56
00:03:17,751 --> 00:03:19,143
miss something important.

57
00:03:19,143 --> 00:03:23,485
The fix for that fear isn't size, it's the natural-unit thinking from the last

58
00:03:23,485 --> 00:03:23,820
slide.

59
00:03:23,820 --> 00:03:28,385
A chunk scoped to one clause, one question, one row, is both focused and complete.

60
00:03:28,385 --> 00:03:33,062
The safety people are reaching for when they oversize a chunk was never about bytes.

61
00:03:33,062 --> 00:03:37,461
It was always about keeping one coherent idea together, and a bigger chunk is a

62
00:03:37,461 --> 00:03:38,741
blunt way to get there.

63
00:03:39,291 --> 00:03:43,884
That's the definition worth carrying forward: a strategy, decided per content type,

64
00:03:43,884 --> 00:03:46,042
not a number picked once and forgotten.

65
00:03:46,042 --> 00:03:48,975
The exercise is the audit most teams have never done.

66
00:03:48,975 --> 00:03:53,457
List what's actually in your corpus, not as one blob but as the distinct kinds of

67
00:03:53,457 --> 00:03:56,611
document it contains, and name the natural unit for each.

68
00:03:56,611 --> 00:04:00,982
If you can't name one, that content type is probably being chunked by a default

69
00:04:00,982 --> 00:04:02,310
nobody chose on purpose.

70
00:04:02,860 --> 00:04:04,505
One thing before you move on.

71
00:04:04,505 --> 00:04:09,214
List the content types feeding your retrieval system, genuinely distinct types, not

72
00:04:09,214 --> 00:04:10,065
just documents.

73
00:04:10,065 --> 00:04:14,718
An FAQ, a policy library, and a product spec are three different chunking problems

74
00:04:14,718 --> 00:04:16,533
wearing the same file extension.

75
00:04:16,533 --> 00:04:21,185
Check each one honestly: if the answer is "whatever the default did," that's not a

76
00:04:21,185 --> 00:04:24,136
decision, it's a gap this lecture just made visible.

77
00:04:24,136 --> 00:04:28,504
Next lecture gives you the metrics to actually measure whether any of this is

78
00:04:28,504 --> 00:04:32,930
working, instead of judging retrieval quality by how a handful of demo queries

79
00:04:32,930 --> 00:04:33,894
happened to look.
